5	## 2. Row 426: why the compiled `cast[\T\]` never matches
6	
7	The fork: whether row 426 fits the batch, which depends on its cause, not traced when the row was opened.
8	
9	`probes/cast/`:
10	- `cast-template-javap.txt`: the compiler library's `cast` template tests `instanceof T`, which the loader rewrites to the instance's class, and then reads the clause's binding `y` as `getstatic fortress/CompilerLibrary$y.ONLY`, a top-level object that does not exist. That is the `NoClassDefFoundError: fortress/CompilerLibrary$y` of the row's own capture.
11	- `CastBind.fss` and `CastBind.comp.txt`: a `typecase` on the function's own static parameter matches at a concrete type on the compiled path: `isT[\ZZ32\](z)`, `isT[\RR64\](r)` and `isT[\String\](s)` answer "is T" for values of those types, and `isT[\ZZ32\](7)` answers "is not T", because a numeral is an `IntLiteral` at run time on this path (row 79). A clause that binds the value, in either spelling (`y: T` as the compiler library writes it, `x': T` as the one library does, `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:33-37`), dies reading `CastBind$x'`.
12	- `CaseBindPlain.fss` and `CaseBindPlain.comp.txt`: the same death with no static parameter anywhere, `w: Wrap => ...` dying on `CaseBindPlain$w`.
13	
14	What it settles: row 426 is two things. One is row 351's typecase site: `CodeGen.forTypecase` never reads a clause's name (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:2116-2147`), and row 351 already records the fix, a local for the clause body in the shape `CodeGen` uses at `:2853` and `:3945`, at this site and at `forTry`'s (`:2020-2061`). The other is that `cast[\ZZ32\](0)` passes a numeral, which is not a `ZZ32` on the compiled path, as the specification has it (`Specification/basic/expressions/literals.tex:127-141`; a clause matches when the value's type is a subtype of the clause's type, `Specification/basic/expressions/typecase.tex:94-95`). Answer 7's identity functions pass numerals through `cast[\T\]` (`additiveIdentity`, `multiplicativeIdentity`), so on the compiled path they need both row 351's fix and branch values that are of type `T` at run time. Both are small; the batch record puts them in rung G.
