23	- `callsite-rebased.patch`: the change's `OverloadSet.java` half (`explorations/reviews/mie-probes/scope/scope-shadow.patch`, its first file) applied to 2851e5086. Seven of its eight hunks apply with offsets of 33 to 35 lines; the eighth adds an import whose context rung Z changed, and was added by hand. The patch keeps the probe's switch, `-Dprobe.scope`, off by default.
24	- Three programs, each compiled and run in the compiler's world stock and with the change as a classpath shadow at `-Dprobe.scope=callsite`: `ScopeAlpha2.fss` and `ScopeZZ32Sub.fss` (copied unchanged from `explorations/reviews/mie-probes/scope/`) and `ScopeArmsLegal.fss` (that directory's `ScopeArms` without its object `Mixed`, the double member route A's checker refuses, so that the stock checker accepts it). Captures `*.stock.txt` and `*.callsite.txt`.
25	- Stock, all three compile and die with `ClassCastException`: `ScopeAlpha2` casts to `Marker⟦Blue,Red⟧`, `ScopeArmsLegal` to the unrewritten `Marker⟦Y⟧`, `ScopeZZ32Sub` to `Sub⟦fortress|CompilerBuiltin%ZZ32⟧`. With the change all three print the specification's answers: `SubM[Red,Blue]`, `TwoM[Red,Blue]`; `SubM[Blue]`, `TagM[Blue]`; `SubM[ZZ32]`.
26	- All three are legal under route A and under answer 9's positional rule: each more specific arm agrees with the less specific one on its static parameters position by position, `ScopeAlpha2`'s only up to renaming (its comment says so).
27	
28	What it settles: the measured fix stands on today's tree for template dispatchers. Its limits are the note's own (section 3, "The remainder"): generic dotted methods, functional methods in a top-level set, `opr` and `nat` parameters, and a dispatcher that is not a template. The last is a legal program since answer 9 allows a generic declaration beside a plain one.
